    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d731">

                <pb facs="0958" n="844" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg844"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/9290: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Warren</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-02-17T10:00:00+09:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 17,
                                1922—10 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 3:15
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>29. Following from Vladivostok:</p>
                <p>“February 16, 6 p.m. Kappel troops<anchor n="68" xml:id="d731-f1"/> 12th forced
                    to retire before Red forces from Habarovsk have taken position at River Amur.
                    Japanese Army apparently much concerned report 10,000 Red against 4,000 White
                    troops. If forced to retire through the, Japanese zone of operation Kappels will
                    undoubtedly be disarmed.[”]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Warren</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d731-f1" n="68" xml:id="d731-f1-content">
                <p>White Russians, taking their name from Gen. V. O. Kappel of the Siberian Army,
                    who died in January 1920 in the retreat following the defeat of Kolchak.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
